-- title:
--   Value and mark truncation in L² for Poisson integrands
-- statement:
--   This auxiliary module comes from the upstream library LevyStochCalc (https://github.com/cgarryZA/LevyStochCalc) and supplies truncation convergence results rather than new definitions. Let $P$ be a probability measure on a measurable space $Ω$, let $ν$ be a σ-finite measure on a measurable mark space $E$, and let $T∈ℝ$; suppose $φ:Ω×ℝ×E→ℝ$ is jointly measurable and its iterated squared integral $\int_Ω\int_{[0,T]}\int_E |φ(ω,s,e)|^2\,dν(e)\,ds\,dP(ω)$ is strictly less than $∞$, with the integrals valued in $[0,∞]$ (`ENNReal`) and $ds$ denoting Lebesgue measure.
--
--   The result `LevyStochCalc.Poisson.Compensated.truncation_L2_converges` states that replacing $φ$ by $\max(-M,\min(M,φ))$, for natural numbers $M→∞$, makes the corresponding iterated squared-error integral tend to zero in $[0,∞]$. The result `LevyStochCalc.Poisson.Compensated.mark_truncation_L2_converges` gives the same convergence for $φ\mathbf{1}_{S_N}$, where $S_N=\bigcup_{m<N}A_m$ and $(A_m)_{m∈ℕ}$ is any sequence of measurable subsets of $E$ whose union is $E$; no finite-measure assumption on these sets is required. The auxiliary results `truncation_pointwise_tendsto` and `truncation_dominated` state, respectively, that the squared clipping error for each real value tends to zero and is bounded by the square of that value.
